In `MauiProgram.cs`, use conditional compilation or the `AddPlatformSpecific` pattern:

```csharp
public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp()
{
    var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
    builder.UseMauiApp<App>();

#if ANDROID
    builder.Services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, Platforms.Android.AndroidNotificationService>();
#elif IOS
    builder.Services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, Platforms.iOS.iOSNotificationService>();
#elif WINDOWS
    builder.Services.AddSingleton<INotificationService, Platforms.Windows.WindowsNotificationService>();
#endif

    return builder.Build();
}
```

Each platform-specific implementation lives under its respective `Platforms/` folder (which is already per-platform compiled by the SDK).

**Key points:**

- The `#if` directives ensure only the relevant implementation is compiled per target, so no missing-type errors occur on other platforms.
- Place each implementation in `Platforms/Android/`, `Platforms/iOS/`, or `Platforms/Windows/` — those folders are already conditionally included via the default `.csproj` settings.
- Define `INotificationService` in shared code (e.g., `Services/INotificationService.cs`).

This is safe because the compiler excludes unreachable branches entirely per platform build.
